course: dictionary
lesson: 5964
title: Words for a day at the theatre
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are about places and trips, like the theatre or the cinema. When you meet an unknown word, look at the words around it, because they are its context. «Big shows in London's West End or New York's Broadway can be expensive, but afternoon shows called matinees... can be cheaper.» The clue is afternoon, so a matinee must be a show held in the afternoon.
explain: The sentence also contrasts matinees with big shows that cost more. Test your meaning by swapping it in: "afternoon shows called afternoon performances can be cheaper." It still makes sense, so your meaning is probably right.
explain: A matinee can be a play, a show or a film. If your family goes to the cinema on a Saturday afternoon, that trip is a matinee. The word is often used for family outings, so you may hear it when people plan a day out.
fact: A matinee is a performance of a play, show or film in the afternoon.
fact: «Matinees» is the plural, which means more than one afternoon show.
fact: Matinees are popular for family outings, because they are held in the daytime.
fact: The sentence says matinees «can be cheaper» than the big evening shows in London or New York.
fact: The word «afternoon» next to «matinees» gives you the meaning, so it is a context clue.
exercise: «Afternoon shows called matinees can be cheaper than big evening shows.» What is a matinee? | A tall theatre in New York | A performance at midnight | *A performance in the afternoon | A ticket that has already sold out
exercise: Which word in the sentence tells you when a matinee happens? | expensive | *afternoon | cheaper | London
exercise: Ella says a matinee is only a football match. Is she right? | Yes, matinees are always sports | Yes, but only in London | No, a matinee is a play, show or film in the afternoon | *No, she is wrong, because a matinee is not a sports match
test: Jayden's family goes to the cinema for a matinee on Saturday. When do they go? | At bedtime | *In the afternoon | At midnight | In the morning
test: Which sentence uses the plural of matinee correctly? | We saw two matinee shows and a matinee | We went to the matinee and the matinee | *We went to two matinees this month | The matinees is at the theatre
exam: Mum says the afternoon matinee is cheaper than the big evening show. What is a matinee, and why might it cost less? | A show that is always free | An evening show that costs more | *An afternoon show, and it can be cheaper | A type of theatre seat only
